.TH qsh-trust-accept 1  "accept 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-trust\-accept \- Dial `address`, redeem `code` against its invite, and — on a successful mutual proof — pin the peer exactly as `trust add` would (ADR\-0002, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh trust accept\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIADDRESS\fR> <\fICODE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Dial `address`, redeem `code` against its invite, and — on a successful mutual proof — pin the peer exactly as `trust add` would (ADR\-0002, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIADDRESS\fR>
`host:port` of the device that printed `code` via `trust invite`
.TP
<\fICODE\fR>
The invite code, as printed (case\-insensitive, hyphens ignored)
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
